# Vulnerability details

## Impact
In statement ```token.safeTransferFrom(vars.stratAddr, address(this), vars.freeWantInStrat - vars.credit);```, if this token is ERC777 (extension of ERC20), receiver can register hook function which will be called when receiver send tokens, so receiver can increase state variable ```totalAllocated``` by re-entering the function ```report```, and then call the ```withdraw``` function to extract more assets with the same share.

## Proof of Concept
1. Enter the function ```report``` for the first time and execute ```token.safeTransferFrom```, which triggers the sender’s ```tokensToSend``` function.
2. In function ```tokensToSend```, sender can call the report function again to increase the ```totalAllocated```. 
3. Then sender call the ```withdraw``` function which will call function ```_withdraw```. In function ```_withdraw```, when calculating the ```value```, due to the increase of ```totalAllocated```, the value returned by ```balance()``` increases, which further causes the value returned by the ```_freeFunds()``` function to increase, resulting in an increase in the final calculated ```value```, which means the sender can extract more assets with the same share.
```
function withdraw(uint256 _shares) external {
    _withdraw(_shares, msg.sender, msg.sender);
}

function _withdraw(
    uint256 _shares,
    address _receiver,
    address _owner
) internal nonReentrant returns (uint256 value) {
    require(_shares != 0, "Invalid amount");
    value = (_freeFunds() * _shares) / totalSupply();
    ······
}

function _freeFunds() internal view returns (uint256) {
    return balance() - _calculateLockedProfit();
}

function balance() public view returns (uint256) {
    return token.balanceOf(address(this)) + totalAllocated;
}

```

